{
    "word": "acherontic",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "b1121c31-66c5-3fee-8f57-24115ca59aff",
                "word": "Acherontic",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1bf46a63-a88e-3510-857d-76d1ee2cc945",
                        "entry_id": "b1121c31-66c5-3fee-8f57-24115ca59aff",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of, pertaining to or resembling Acheron (one of the rivers located in the underworld according to ancient Greek mythology)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "95079854-0885-4130-a3aa-9a2736e6acb2",
                                "sense_id": "1bf46a63-a88e-3510-857d-76d1ee2cc945",
                                "text": "It was a Comedy, to see what a crowding (as if it had bene at a newe Play,) there was vpon the Acheronticque Strond,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "47480f7c-391a-4de4-bdcd-59bc9312edeb",
                                "sense_id": "1bf46a63-a88e-3510-857d-76d1ee2cc945",
                                "text": "Fierce earthquakes tear the world, the heavens bow,\nA passage opens to the shades below:\nFrom acherontick shores black fiends ascend,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b772163-7999-418a-8ab0-3a1e6052a1e4",
                                "sense_id": "1bf46a63-a88e-3510-857d-76d1ee2cc945",
                                "text": "Is Free Industry free to convert all our rivers into Acherontic sewers; England generally into a roaring sooty smith’s forge?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "07755991-b441-4a85-8621-5cdf1102cedd",
                                "sense_id": "1bf46a63-a88e-3510-857d-76d1ee2cc945",
                                "text": "Although Wright’s underwater man in Venice may remind us of the ghosts of the drowned in the Ohio River, this Italian fantasy is more benign; the canal is not like the Acherontic Ohio […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "626323e9-e9e6-33c1-ab40-4330c0f37c90",
                        "entry_id": "b1121c31-66c5-3fee-8f57-24115ca59aff",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or pertaining to hell."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4fb9d072-72f2-4880-a944-de61c4cb2083",
                                "sense_id": "626323e9-e9e6-33c1-ab40-4330c0f37c90",
                                "text": "1623, George Langford, Search the Scriptures, London: John Clarke, Section 7, p. 43,\nHow did those Aegyptians storme, when Moses and Aaron, Crumwell and Cranmer came, to deliuer Gods Israel, from that Acheronticall ignorance?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f9b5febf-2a14-46fa-a2c9-635e2201e563",
                                "sense_id": "626323e9-e9e6-33c1-ab40-4330c0f37c90",
                                "text": "Both sex, hideously cut, and gash, and pink in sundry works, their browes, nose, cheeks, armes, brest, back, belly, thighes and legges in Acherontick order: in a word, are so deformed, that if they had studied to become antick, they might be praised for invention."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3bd934b4-fff3-4bb4-ad4a-a4974995f25a",
                                "sense_id": "626323e9-e9e6-33c1-ab40-4330c0f37c90",
                                "text": "[…] they proceeded through the fog like Acherontic shades for a long while, without sound or gesture."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e6414d1f-f301-33df-ac14-44b914c55f37",
                        "entry_id": "b1121c31-66c5-3fee-8f57-24115ca59aff",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Lacking joy and comfort; nearing death."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "30a894fd-7df7-4603-a602-712de3c8bfec",
                                "sense_id": "e6414d1f-f301-33df-ac14-44b914c55f37",
                                "text": "It was twilight in the streets. On every corner glowed lights from doors and dusty windows. Acherontic figures lounged by lazily or sat against the walls."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "08e32edc-8470-4032-8376-9e2ee3391f6b",
                                "sense_id": "e6414d1f-f301-33df-ac14-44b914c55f37",
                                "text": "1599, John Weever, Epigrammes in the Oldest Cut, and Newest Fashion, London: Thomas Bushell, The Thirde Weeke, Epig. 7,\nDepart to blacke nights Acheronticke Cell,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "47051575-6fff-4bbf-a749-faff138ea27e",
                                "sense_id": "e6414d1f-f301-33df-ac14-44b914c55f37",
                                "text": "1621, Robert Burton, The Anatomy of Melancholy, Oxford: Henry Cripps, Part 3, Section 3, Member 4, Subsection 2, p. 701,\n[…] it is most odious, when an old Acheronticke dizard, that hath one foote in his graue, shall flicker after a young wench, what can be more detestable."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3359ca53-85a5-4c11-b26d-cc878b2859ef",
                                "sense_id": "e6414d1f-f301-33df-ac14-44b914c55f37",
                                "text": "I see no owls, though I am told that at night they fill these Acherontic woods with demon hooting […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7d65b5f5-edc7-4eb5-8b01-366612f84dd0",
                                "sense_id": "e6414d1f-f301-33df-ac14-44b914c55f37",
                                "text": "In Manchester, our designer Roy Stonehouse had built the dark lanes of his [Dickens’] Acherontic township on the low-lying land behind Water Street […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
